WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.914
Types, parts and fields do not have to be built in the admin.

00:00:04.914 --> 00:00:09.061
A module can define them in code, so that every site that enables

00:00:09.061 --> 00:00:10.638
the module gets them.

00:00:10.638 --> 00:00:14.610
This video shows how, with a data migration and with a recipe.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.762
Content definitions built in the admin live in the database of one site.

00:00:23.762 --> 00:00:27.731
A module that needs a Product type, or an SEO part, cannot ask

00:00:27.731 --> 00:00:30.123
every site to rebuild them by hand.

00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:33.800
It defines them in code, and they travel with the module.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.985
Two tools do this:

00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:40.711
a data migration, which is a C# class that alters definitions through the

00:00:40.711 --> 00:00:46.333
content definition manager, and a recipe, which is a JSON file that a migration can run.

00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:50.460
The migration is versioned, so each site catches up on its own.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.436
The SEO module is a complete example.

00:00:57.436 --> 00:01:02.666
Its manifest lists the modules it depends on, among them Media, whose field

00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:07.380
it uses, and Recipes Core Services, which provides IRecipeMigrator.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.610
Orchard Core enables them with the module, and runs their migrations first.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.021
Startup registers the migration with AddDataMigration.

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:21.962
Orchard Core does not discover migrations by itself, so a migration that is

00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:23.914
not registered never runs.

00:01:23.914 --> 00:01:27.880
Startup also registers the part class, with AddContentPart.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.642
The migration inherits from DataMigration.

00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:40.742
Dependency injection provides IContentDefinitionManager, which reads and writes definitions, and IRecipeMigrator, which runs a recipe file.

00:01:40.742 --> 00:01:43.440
The steps of the migration come next.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.937
AlterTypeDefinitionAsync takes the name of a type and a builder.

00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:56.725
It creates the type when it does not exist, and changes it when it does.

00:01:56.725 --> 00:02:03.170
The builder sets the settings of the type, such as Creatable, Draftable, Versionable and Securable.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.989
WithPart attaches a part by name.

00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:09.968
A second builder configures the part in the context of this type, for

00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:12.143
example its position in the editor.

00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:15.750
Names start with a letter and contain only letters and digits.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.715
A type created without a display name is shown as its name with

00:02:20.715 --> 00:02:24.442
a space before each capital letter, as the slide shows.

00:02:24.442 --> 00:02:27.130
WithDisplayName chooses another label.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.788
AlterPartDefinitionAsync creates or changes a part.

00:02:34.788 --> 00:02:38.792
Attachable lets editors add the part to a type in the admin.

00:02:38.792 --> 00:02:40.552
WithField adds a field:

00:02:40.552 --> 00:02:46.740
OfType names its field type, WithDisplayName sets its label, and WithSettings gives it settings.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.512
A type has no fields of its own.

00:02:49.512 --> 00:02:52.971
Create a part with the name of the type, attach it, and add

00:02:52.971 --> 00:02:56.537
the fields to that part; the admin lists them under the type.

00:02:56.537 --> 00:02:59.730
An added field needs OfType, or saving the part fails.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.810
When a definition is long, put it in a recipe.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.078
The file goes in the Migrations folder of the module.

00:03:10.078 --> 00:03:15.450
The migration runs it with IRecipeMigrator, passing the file name and the migration itself.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.753
This recipe belongs to the SEO module.

00:03:18.753 --> 00:03:22.061
Its first step lists ContentTypes and ContentParts:

00:03:22.061 --> 00:03:26.240
the Social Meta Settings type, and the part that holds its fields.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.360
A deployment plan can export definitions as these same two lists.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.622
Give every type in a recipe a DisplayName.

00:03:33.622 --> 00:03:37.338
The step applies the display name and the settings of a type only

00:03:37.338 --> 00:03:42.100
when the DisplayName is present, while the parts of the type are attached either way.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.478
The Content Types feature provides three recipe steps for definitions.

00:03:47.478 --> 00:03:54.115
ContentDefinition adds and updates, ReplaceContentDefinition deletes each type and part it lists before

00:03:54.115 --> 00:03:58.622
creating it again, and DeleteContentDefinition removes them by name.

00:03:58.622 --> 00:04:01.910
Deleting a part also removes it from every type.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:11.160
Each method returns the version it leaves the site at, and Orchard Core

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.717
stores that number for each site.

00:04:13.717 --> 00:04:19.292
A site without one runs CreateAsync, and any other runs only the later methods.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:24.210
Here CreateAsync returns 2, so a fresh tenant skips UpdateFrom1Async.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.881
To change a definition later, add an UpdateFrom method for the current version,

00:04:29.881 --> 00:04:31.792
and return the next number.

00:04:31.792 --> 00:04:36.945
A site never runs a method twice, so editing a released one changes nothing for it.

00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:40.940
CreateAsync can carry the change too, for fresh sites, as here.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.721
An alteration starts from the stored definition and changes only what it names,

00:04:46.721 --> 00:04:48.260
so it never removes.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.700
RemovePart takes a part from a type, RemoveField takes a field from a

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:58.560
part, and DeleteTypeDefinitionAsync and DeletePartDefinitionAsync delete a definition itself.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.205
Let's see the SEO migration run.

00:05:05.205 --> 00:05:09.031
On the Features page, search for SEO, and select Enable.

00:05:09.031 --> 00:05:14.277
Orchard Core reloads the tenant, and the migrations of the enabled features run

00:05:14.277 --> 00:05:15.820
as it starts again.

00:05:16.318 --> 00:05:21.744
Under Design, Content Definition, Content Parts, the SeoMeta part is listed, with the

00:05:21.744 --> 00:05:24.428
description that the migration gave it.

00:05:24.895 --> 00:05:26.409
Let's edit the part.

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:31.475
Its three Media fields, Default social image, Open graph image and Twitter image,

00:05:31.475 --> 00:05:36.075
are the ones AlterPartDefinitionAsync added, with the same display names.

00:05:36.529 --> 00:05:40.076
Under Content Types, Social Meta Settings comes from the recipe.

00:05:40.076 --> 00:05:41.089
Let's edit it.

00:05:41.089 --> 00:05:44.738
Its Fields come from the part named like the type, which the Parts

00:05:44.738 --> 00:05:47.829
list marks as Contains the fields for the current type.

00:05:52.029 --> 00:05:56.652
To use the values in code, write a class that inherits from ContentPart,

00:05:56.652 --> 00:05:58.194
named like the part.

00:05:58.194 --> 00:06:02.758
For each field, add a property with the name and the type of the field.

00:06:02.758 --> 00:06:06.789
DefaultSocialImage is a MediaField, as the migration declares.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:10.061
A part handler receives the typed part.

00:06:10.061 --> 00:06:13.648
Anywhere else, GetOrCreate returns it from a content item.

00:06:13.648 --> 00:06:17.964
Editors can change definitions in the admin, so a property can be null,

00:06:17.964 --> 00:06:20.599
and your code should check it before use.

00:06:22.599 --> 00:06:26.108
That is how a module carries its content definitions:

00:06:26.108 --> 00:06:30.630
migrations in C#, recipes in JSON, and a version stored for each site.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:34.972
Define them once, and every site that enables the module gets them.

00:06:34.972 --> 00:06:36.519
Thanks for watching.

